WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.700 --> 00:00:02.035
When you think of great films

00:00:02.035 --> 00:00:06.573
you might think of their directors 
like Spielberg, Hitchcock, Peele

00:00:06.573 --> 00:00:10.877
or actors like Meryl Streep, 
Viola Davis, Morgan Freeman.

00:00:11.211 --> 00:00:13.713
But recently, there's 
a new name in the mix

00:00:13.713 --> 00:00:16.116
and it's not a person 
but a studio.

00:00:16.116 --> 00:00:17.317
A24.

00:00:21.755 --> 00:00:23.390
A24 movies have been nominated

00:00:23.390 --> 00:00:26.259
for over 50 Oscars and 
have won 16.

00:00:26.526 --> 00:00:29.696
They've taken over Halloween 
and maybe your closet.

00:00:29.996 --> 00:00:31.698
And the reason why 
you know their name

00:00:31.698 --> 00:00:33.133
is part of their strategy.

00:00:33.133 --> 00:00:36.970
A strategy that's helped take A24 
from a tiny distribution company...

00:00:37.203 --> 00:00:39.773
to making some of the biggest 
and weirdest movies

00:00:39.773 --> 00:00:42.208
and TV shows of the past ten years.

00:00:46.946 --> 00:00:49.082
I've maybe seen  
more A24 films

00:00:49.082 --> 00:00:51.051
than the people who work 
at A24.

00:00:51.051 --> 00:00:52.018
My name is Nate Jones.

00:00:52.018 --> 00:00:54.320
I'm a senior writer for Vulture 
in New York Magazine.

00:00:54.354 --> 00:00:59.659
Last August, 8/24, I ranked all of them 
from worst to best.

00:01:00.226 --> 00:01:03.930
The company was founded on two sort of 
basic creative principles.

00:01:03.930 --> 00:01:06.032
The first was that they were going 
to give directors

00:01:06.032 --> 00:01:08.468
an almost unprecedented 
creative freedom

00:01:08.468 --> 00:01:12.238
and then to pay for that, because that is, 
you know, creatively risky.

00:01:12.238 --> 00:01:15.508
They basically decided they weren't 
going to be spending money

00:01:15.508 --> 00:01:17.177
on traditional forms of marketing.

00:01:17.177 --> 00:01:18.878
They were going to try and use

00:01:18.878 --> 00:01:20.713
viral marketing and 
word of mouth.

00:01:20.713 --> 00:01:23.550
These cheaper ways of bringing attention 
to their movies.

00:01:24.551 --> 00:01:27.120
So the first half of their existence,
they only bought. 

00:01:27.120 --> 00:01:28.621
They didn't make any of the films.

00:01:29.589 --> 00:01:33.493
In other words, A24 was founded 
as a distribution company

00:01:33.493 --> 00:01:35.061
and as your resident film nerd

00:01:35.061 --> 00:01:36.296
I have to give you a quick lesson

00:01:36.296 --> 00:01:38.631
on the moviemaking pipeline 
for this all to make sense.

00:01:38.798 --> 00:01:40.567
So bear with me for a second.

00:01:41.501 --> 00:01:43.503
First, there's production 
where the movie gets made.

00:01:43.636 --> 00:01:44.904
Then there's distribution

00:01:44.904 --> 00:01:46.673
where a company buys the rights 
to finished film

00:01:46.673 --> 00:01:48.007
and takes on the work of marketing it

00:01:48.007 --> 00:01:49.275
and making deals to connect it

00:01:49.275 --> 00:01:51.144
to companies that can show it 
to audiences.

00:01:51.144 --> 00:01:52.979
That's the third part 
known as exhibition

00:01:52.979 --> 00:01:55.281
where companies like movie theaters 
and streaming services

00:01:55.281 --> 00:01:56.983
show movies for the general public.

00:01:57.450 --> 00:01:58.885
The studios you’re likely familiar with

00:01:58.885 --> 00:02:01.621
generally take on both 
production and distribution.

00:02:01.988 --> 00:02:04.757
So something like Indiana Jones
and the Dial of Destiny

00:02:04.757 --> 00:02:06.159
is produced by Disney.

00:02:06.159 --> 00:02:08.428
And then Disney also makes 
the trailers, posters

00:02:08.428 --> 00:02:10.530
and deals with with exhibitors 
like AMC and Regal

00:02:10.530 --> 00:02:12.165
to get that movie shown in theaters.

00:02:12.165 --> 00:02:14.534
But smaller, independently produced films

00:02:14.567 --> 00:02:17.337
often go to film festivals like 
Sundance or Tribeca

00:02:17.337 --> 00:02:19.105
in hopes of getting the attention 
of a distributor

00:02:19.105 --> 00:02:20.373
who will buy the rights to the film

00:02:20.373 --> 00:02:22.142
and will help them make 
those connections.

00:02:23.676 --> 00:02:26.346
In the beginning, 
A24's sole focus

00:02:26.346 --> 00:02:29.883
was to find great indie movies and 
buy the distribution rights for them.

00:02:29.983 --> 00:02:31.985
It's just easier that way. 
You don't need as much money.

00:02:32.185 --> 00:02:34.554
You know, it takes a lot of cash 
to produce a film.

00:02:34.587 --> 00:02:36.956
Doesn't take as much to just buy a film 
that's already made.

00:02:37.223 --> 00:02:39.993
Let’s use Spring Breakers, 
their first hit as a case study

00:02:39.993 --> 00:02:42.562
to show how all their strategies come together.

00:02:42.896 --> 00:02:45.398
Spring Breakers was the first 
A24 film I saw.

00:02:45.465 --> 00:02:47.600
It was the first A24 film
most people saw.

00:02:47.600 --> 00:02:51.504
I think it was a very good introduction 
to the A24 style.

00:02:51.504 --> 00:02:53.506
The film has an extremely strong

00:02:53.506 --> 00:02:55.942
artistic vision from director 
Harmony Korine...

00:02:56.075 --> 00:03:01.047
who A24 courted by making bespoke 
gun-shaped bongs.

00:03:01.047 --> 00:03:03.483
The movie had striking 
visual choices

00:03:03.483 --> 00:03:05.718
like this rain that almost 
looks like blood...

00:03:06.019 --> 00:03:09.756
and a neon color palette
that has become a staple of A24 films.

00:03:10.156 --> 00:03:11.558
It had a clear hook.

00:03:11.558 --> 00:03:15.628
You have these sort of Disney starlets 
like Selena Gomez

00:03:15.628 --> 00:03:18.531
and they're, you know, getting up to 
no good and doing drugs.

00:03:18.565 --> 00:03:20.200
And if you were a young person

00:03:20.200 --> 00:03:21.701
who'd grown up on the Disney Channel

00:03:21.701 --> 00:03:24.837
suddenly all these people you'd seen 
in this very squeaky clean entertainment

00:03:24.837 --> 00:03:27.574
were getting very rough and grimy.

00:03:27.707 --> 00:03:31.311
And on top of that, it had supremely 
memeable sequences.

00:03:31.444 --> 00:03:34.614
James Franco has, you know, 
the first of many A24 scenes

00:03:34.614 --> 00:03:37.050
that would just become this sort of 
perfect little tidbit.

00:03:37.083 --> 00:03:39.352
—He has this speech. 
—[overlapping] Look at my shit.

00:03:39.719 --> 00:03:41.988
I. got short

00:03:41.988 --> 00:03:44.958
Every fucking color.
Scarface on repeat.

00:03:45.091 --> 00:03:47.760
It was just this 
weird sort of thing

00:03:47.760 --> 00:03:49.829
that really had just caught on.

00:03:50.563 --> 00:03:52.665
Their innovative online 
marketing strategy

00:03:52.665 --> 00:03:55.301
leveraged gifts and memes 
to make the movie buzzy.

00:03:55.501 --> 00:03:57.203
Like this one that amassed over

00:03:57.203 --> 00:04:00.073
20,000 thumbs up 
on Facebook which...

00:04:00.974 --> 00:04:02.809
you know, doesn't sound 
like a lot now.

00:04:02.809 --> 00:04:06.212
But you got to remember, this was 
2013 back when the LA Times

00:04:06.212 --> 00:04:08.448
was calling likes thumbs ups.

00:04:08.848 --> 00:04:10.617
Anyways, the marketing worked.

00:04:10.617 --> 00:04:13.453
The movie ended up setting 
per screen attendance records

00:04:13.453 --> 00:04:14.654
for its opening weekend

00:04:14.654 --> 00:04:17.190
as well as clocking the biggest 
premiere of the movie

00:04:17.190 --> 00:04:19.092
in limited release that year.

00:04:19.592 --> 00:04:20.793
The second weekend...

00:04:20.793 --> 00:04:22.929
everybody talked a lot about 
how they hated the movie...

00:04:23.062 --> 00:04:25.665
but their first weekend was incredible.

00:04:25.898 --> 00:04:27.867
That movie was a huge 
word of mouth hit.

00:04:27.867 --> 00:04:29.202
If you look at how much money

00:04:29.202 --> 00:04:30.670
it made compared to other films

00:04:30.670 --> 00:04:31.971
you know, it doesn't really stand out.

00:04:31.971 --> 00:04:34.007
But if you look at how much money it makes 
compared to other...

00:04:34.641 --> 00:04:37.877
independent films from that era, 
like it was a huge hit.

00:04:38.011 --> 00:04:41.881
And crucially, it proved that 
the A24 method worked.

00:04:42.081 --> 00:04:45.151
If they could keep curating distinct, 
vibey movies

00:04:45.151 --> 00:04:48.488
marketing them online for less money 
and more virality

00:04:48.488 --> 00:04:50.156
and distributing them in a way

00:04:50.156 --> 00:04:52.292
where the box office 
can rival the budget...

00:04:52.558 --> 00:04:54.360
then they were in business.

00:04:54.360 --> 00:04:57.397
And oh boy, were they in business.

00:04:58.998 --> 00:05:01.000
They were able to create such a strong brand.

00:05:01.067 --> 00:05:04.470
You knew in your head kind of 
what an A24 film was.

00:05:04.470 --> 00:05:06.539
And I think that's down to 
their sense of taste.

00:05:06.639 --> 00:05:08.808
I don't know of any other studio
that has such an almost

00:05:08.808 --> 00:05:11.844
like a personal style in that way.

00:05:12.211 --> 00:05:14.547
They helped launch careers 
for Ari Aster

00:05:14.547 --> 00:05:16.149
the Daniels and Robert Eggers.

00:05:16.282 --> 00:05:18.651
And afforded directorial debuts

00:05:18.651 --> 00:05:21.154
to Greta Gerwig, Jonah Hill, 
and Bo Burnham.

00:05:21.254 --> 00:05:23.022
They would find these up and coming 
directors and be like

00:05:23.022 --> 00:05:25.658
we are going to sort of usher you 
to the next level.

00:05:26.059 --> 00:05:28.661
Ex-Machina showed their shrewdness in marketing

00:05:28.661 --> 00:05:30.330
as they created a Tinder account

00:05:30.330 --> 00:05:32.565
for Alicia Vikander's lead character...

00:05:32.732 --> 00:05:34.567
inviting men to watch the movie.

00:05:34.567 --> 00:05:37.837
And films like Room became 
big hits at the Oscars.

00:05:38.671 --> 00:05:41.007
They won their first Oscars 
in 2016.

00:05:41.507 --> 00:05:43.876
They won best actress for 
Brie Larson and Room.

00:05:44.043 --> 00:05:46.112
They won best visual effects
for Ex Machina.

00:05:46.112 --> 00:05:48.114
And they won Best Documentary for Amy.

00:05:48.147 --> 00:05:50.249
So they were they had been on the scene, 
they had been respected.

00:05:50.516 --> 00:05:53.586
They had sort of known how to play 
the industry game.

00:05:53.586 --> 00:05:54.954
You know, they weren't too cool for it.

00:05:54.954 --> 00:05:56.589
They weren't holding their heads above it.

00:05:56.856 --> 00:05:58.024
And four years in

00:05:58.024 --> 00:06:01.427
after curating a strong brand 
off of other people's movies...

00:06:01.627 --> 00:06:03.663
they decided to produce 
one on their own.

00:06:03.830 --> 00:06:06.599
So Moonlight was the first film 
that they made themselves.

00:06:06.866 --> 00:06:08.368
There was a bet on Barry Jenkins

00:06:08.368 --> 00:06:11.504
who had made only one other feature 
and eight years before this point...

00:06:11.804 --> 00:06:14.040
and they basically said, like,

00:06:14.040 --> 00:06:15.541
we will let you do

00:06:15.541 --> 00:06:18.344
totally whatever you want and 
we will support you visually.

00:06:18.511 --> 00:06:20.680
It does look a lot like 
other A24 films.

00:06:20.680 --> 00:06:22.148
You know, you have that neon

00:06:22.148 --> 00:06:23.950
you have that very subjective lighting.

00:06:23.950 --> 00:06:26.119
Moonlight — that was when they get 
the reputation, I think

00:06:26.119 --> 00:06:28.554
for like, oh, you know, 
it's not just online hype.

00:06:28.588 --> 00:06:29.822
They're not just like buzzy.

00:06:29.822 --> 00:06:32.258
They are going to release 
some great, great films.

00:06:32.458 --> 00:06:34.660
If you look at the films they 
have produced since Moonlight

00:06:34.660 --> 00:06:35.595
it's a lot of the movies

00:06:35.595 --> 00:06:39.332
we think of as like very-A24:
Uncut Gems.

00:06:39.332 --> 00:06:40.800
It's Everything Everywhere All At Once.

00:06:40.800 --> 00:06:45.071
You know, it's these movies that are 
these big, bold brand statements.

00:06:45.071 --> 00:06:47.673
In 2017, they expanded to TV.

00:06:48.074 --> 00:06:49.876
When you talk to people 
who watch Euphoria

00:06:49.876 --> 00:06:52.445
a lot of them don't know 
that it's an A24 series...

00:06:52.712 --> 00:06:54.313
because Euphoria airs on HBO

00:06:54.313 --> 00:06:56.849
which is a very strong brand, 
a very big brand.

00:06:56.849 --> 00:06:59.218
And that kind of crowds out the A24 of it all.

00:06:59.552 --> 00:07:02.989
But Beef airs on Netflix, which has 
a much smaller brand.

00:07:03.356 --> 00:07:04.190
And, you know,

00:07:04.190 --> 00:07:07.360
suddenly that allows the A24 of it all 
to kind of jump out.

00:07:07.593 --> 00:07:11.597
And as a cherry on top, they make merch 
for themselves

00:07:11.631 --> 00:07:14.400
not just for the films, you know, 
they will sell A24

00:07:14.400 --> 00:07:17.136
T-shirts and A24 hoodies with 
that little logo on it.

00:07:17.136 --> 00:07:19.972
And that sort of helps build 
that sort of cult around

00:07:19.972 --> 00:07:22.442
this, you know, editorial sensibility that they have.

00:07:22.542 --> 00:07:25.411
And they employ a lot of the techniques
that the fashion industry has too. 

00:07:25.411 --> 00:07:26.412
You know they will do

00:07:26.412 --> 00:07:28.781
collaborations with, you know, 
online ceramics.

00:07:28.981 --> 00:07:30.917
They will do with limited edition drops

00:07:30.917 --> 00:07:32.885
where you've got to get your hands 
on something now. 

00:07:32.885 --> 00:07:35.188
They build this sense of 
exclusivity around it

00:07:35.188 --> 00:07:36.589
where it becomes, you know, there are

00:07:36.589 --> 00:07:37.990
there are A24 merch heads

00:07:37.990 --> 00:07:40.326
you know, who are really hoping to get
 the latest stuff.

00:07:40.393 --> 00:07:44.163
It's something that no other indie studio 
has quite managed to pull off.

00:07:45.832 --> 00:07:48.334
When you look at their trajectory 
in retrospect

00:07:48.334 --> 00:07:49.769
it's easy to be dazzled

00:07:49.769 --> 00:07:51.938
into thinking that they've got 
the Midas touch.

00:07:52.171 --> 00:07:54.273
And in some ways they do.

00:07:54.273 --> 00:07:56.108
—But... 
—We don't need to build them up too much.

00:07:56.108 --> 00:07:58.845
Like any studio, they make 
bad films too.

00:07:58.845 --> 00:08:00.279
You just might not notice it

00:08:00.279 --> 00:08:03.149
because A24 puts out a ton 
of movies every year

00:08:03.149 --> 00:08:05.918
as compared to other studios, 
both big and small.

00:08:06.252 --> 00:08:09.622
In 2022, the studio put out 
20 films.

00:08:09.989 --> 00:08:11.491
That's 2 more than Paramount

00:08:11.491 --> 00:08:13.860
a studio that's way larger than they are.

00:08:14.227 --> 00:08:15.361
But they have a unique way

00:08:15.361 --> 00:08:17.763
of hiding their bad films and 
making them go away.

00:08:17.763 --> 00:08:18.931
They had a deal with DirecTV.

00:08:18.931 --> 00:08:21.467
They now have a deal with Apple TV where

00:08:21.467 --> 00:08:22.835
you know, movies that maybe

00:08:22.835 --> 00:08:25.304
they don't think are quite pass muster.

00:08:25.738 --> 00:08:26.739
They'll just go straight there.

00:08:26.739 --> 00:08:28.674
You know, those ones won't play 
at the metrograph.

00:08:28.674 --> 00:08:29.942
Those ones will be playing at

00:08:29.942 --> 00:08:31.277
you know, your cool indie theater.

00:08:31.277 --> 00:08:32.612
They will kind of

00:08:32.612 --> 00:08:35.114
be quietly shuffled off to, 
you know, DirectTV.

00:08:35.615 --> 00:08:37.049
Maybe people will find them.

00:08:37.049 --> 00:08:39.919
But if people don't find them,
you know, maybe that's okay, too.

00:08:40.353 --> 00:08:42.722
You look at the list of A24 films, 
there's dozens of them

00:08:42.722 --> 00:08:45.491
that you have never heard of that
nobody has ever heard of.

00:08:45.758 --> 00:08:47.293
The Adderall Diaries

00:08:47.293 --> 00:08:48.461
The Kill Team.

00:08:48.461 --> 00:08:49.795
Revenge of the Green Dragons.

00:08:50.129 --> 00:08:52.431
These are movies that truly do not exist.

00:08:52.899 --> 00:08:55.101
But their missteps don't seem to matter much

00:08:55.101 --> 00:08:59.539
when they're tried and true strategies
create such big wins.

00:08:59.906 --> 00:09:02.909
When I talk to the fans last year

00:09:02.942 --> 00:09:04.544
some of them readily admitted, 
oh yeah, like

00:09:04.544 --> 00:09:06.178
I'm in the cult, I'm deep 
in the cult.

00:09:06.178 --> 00:09:08.214
And when I ask like, I asked them why

00:09:08.247 --> 00:09:09.649
the key thing was difference.

00:09:09.649 --> 00:09:12.919
There's so much these days that 
feels sort of the same

00:09:12.919 --> 00:09:15.087
feels very corporate, very controlled

00:09:15.087 --> 00:09:16.923
that that was kind of the one thing

00:09:16.923 --> 00:09:18.157
that they all kind of agreed on

00:09:18.157 --> 00:09:20.259
is that like when they go see an A24 film

00:09:20.259 --> 00:09:21.661
you know, it might be good.

00:09:21.661 --> 00:09:24.363
There's a chance, it might be terrible, 
but, you know,

00:09:24.363 --> 00:09:26.132
they know it's going to be

00:09:26.132 --> 00:09:28.401
just like a little weirder, 
a little more offbeat.

00:09:28.401 --> 00:09:31.837
Ideally, it's going to be something 
they haven't seen before.

00:09:31.837 --> 00:09:32.872
The name means nothing.

00:09:32.872 --> 00:09:35.174
The name is there's no symbolism.

00:09:35.174 --> 00:09:37.710
The name is an Italian highway 
that leads out of Rome...

00:09:38.077 --> 00:09:40.012
that I think Katz was driving on.

00:09:40.012 --> 00:09:42.148
And he was like, oh, that would be 
a good name for a company.

00:09:42.481 --> 00:09:43.716
But it sort of fits, right?

00:09:43.716 --> 00:09:45.618
Because it's abstract.

00:09:45.618 --> 00:09:47.186
It’s sort of mysterious, 
and you're like, Oh,

00:09:47.186 --> 00:09:48.921
there must be a hidden meaning there. 
You know?

00:09:48.921 --> 00:09:50.990
I often like some of the films, 
you know, you're like...

00:09:51.324 --> 00:09:52.625
you know, there must be something there.

00:09:52.625 --> 00:09:54.060
It sparks of curiosity, in a way.

